/** @page xmlschemavalidation Schema Validation

    @tableofcontents

    The xml module validates documents against <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-1/">XML Schema 1.0</a> (XSD;
    Parts <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-1/">1</a> and <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema-2/">2</a>),
    <a href="https://relaxng.org/spec-20011203.html">RELAX NG</a> and
    <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/xml/#dt-doctype">XML
    1.0 DTD</a> schemas. A document can be
    validated while it is parsed into %Qore data, as a DOM document, or while it is read as a stream.

    @section xmlschemavalidationapi Validation APIs

    |!Schema language|!Parse into %Qore data|!DOM document|!Streaming
    |XML Schema 1.0|@ref Qore::Xml::parse_xml_with_schema() "parse_xml_with_schema()"|@ref \
        Qore::Xml::XmlDoc::validateSchema() "XmlDoc::validateSchema()", \
        @ref Qore::Xml::XmlDoc::validateSchemaFile() "XmlDoc::validateSchemaFile()"|\
        @ref Qore::Xml::XmlReader::schemaValidate() "XmlReader::schemaValidate()", \
        @ref Qore::Xml::XmlReader::schemaValidateString() "XmlReader::schemaValidateString()", or the \c xsd \
        constructor option
    |RELAX NG|@ref Qore::Xml::parse_xml_with_relaxng() "parse_xml_with_relaxng()"|@ref \
        Qore::Xml::XmlDoc::validateRelaxNG() "XmlDoc::validateRelaxNG()"|\
        @ref Qore::Xml::XmlReader::relaxNGValidate() "XmlReader::relaxNGValidate()"
    |DTD|@ref Qore::Xml::parse_xml_with_dtd() "parse_xml_with_dtd()"|n/a|n/a

    XSD and RELAX NG validation depend on libxml2 features selected when the module was built; see the
    @ref xmloptionconstants "module option constants".

    The streaming APIs validate each node as it is read, so large documents can be validated without building a DOM
    tree; see @ref xmlcookbookvalidate "the cookbook" for examples of each form.

    @section xmlschemavalidationresources Schema Resources and Sandboxing

    A schema can include and import other schema documents:
    - a schema given as text has no base URI, so relative \c xsd:include and \c xsd:import locations cannot be
      resolved; use a schema file or URI (@ref Qore::Xml::XmlDoc::validateSchemaFile() "XmlDoc::validateSchemaFile()"
      or @ref Qore::Xml::XmlReader::schemaValidate() "XmlReader::schemaValidate()") or a resource callback
    - relative locations are resolved against the including schema's location, including the final URI after HTTP
      redirects
    - an @ref Qore::Xml::AbstractXmlIoInputCallback "AbstractXmlIoInputCallback" object can serve schema resources
      from memory or any other source; it is called before any other loader, and returning @ref nothing "NOTHING"
      falls back to the normal, checked loader
    - \c http:// and \c https:// resources are retrieved with %Qore's HTTP client; HTTPS verifies the server
      certificate and host name against %Qore's OpenSSL trust store
    - file, catalog and network access, including redirect destinations, are checked against the calling
      %Program's sandbox policy: a denied access raises \c ILLEGAL-FILESYSTEM-ACCESS, \c FILESYSTEM-ACCESS-DENIED,
      \c ILLEGAL-NETWORK-ACCESS or \c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED, and never falls back to an unchecked loader

    @section xmlschemavalidationentities Entities and Untrusted Documents

    References to internal parsed entities declared in a document's internal DTD subset are expanded (XML 1.0
    section 4.4.3). External entities are never loaded: a reference to an external general or parameter entity fails
    the parse with an error naming the entity's URI, which prevents XML external entity (XXE) attacks. Entity
    expansion is bounded, so a "billion laughs" document fails with an error. See @ref xmlentityreferences.

    @section xmlschemavalidationconformance XML Schema Conformance

    The module follows XML Schema 1.0 (Second Edition) Parts 1 and 2. Its libxml2 dependency is checked when the
    module is configured, and the private copy of libxml2 that the module can build includes corrections for
    conformance defects of the upstream releases, among them:
    - identity: \c ID, \c IDREF and \c IDREFS bindings, including unresolved references and IDs identifying distinct
      elements; \c xs:key, \c xs:unique and \c xs:keyref tables and nil identities
    - particles: unique particle attribution with counted repetitions, exact finite occurrence bounds (also above
      native integer ranges), and consistent element declarations
    - element substitution groups and blocked derivations, \c finalDefault on anonymous simple types, and wildcard
      \c xsi:type assessment
    - value spaces and canonical forms: \c float and \c double, \c decimal and integer types, calendar values with
      exact fractional seconds and extended years, durations, binary types, \c anyURI, \c QName and \c NOTATION;
      default and fixed values compare typed values
    - the \c Name, \c NCName, \c QName and \c NMTOKEN datatypes, and the types derived from them, use the name
      characters of XML 1.0 Second Edition, as XML Schema 1.0 requires, for every SOAP version

    The following functions give access to XSD lexical rules:
    - @ref Qore::Xml::normalize_xsd_uri() "normalize_xsd_uri()": validates an \c anyURI value and returns its
      whitespace-normalized spelling
    - @ref Qore::Xml::convert_xsd_float() "convert_xsd_float()": converts an XSD \c float or \c double lexical value
      independently of the locale
    - @ref Qore::Xml::canonical_xsd_float() "canonical_xsd_float()": returns the canonical spelling of an XSD \c
      float or \c double value

    @section xmlschemavalidationlibxml2 libxml2 Selection

    The CMake option \c QORE_XML_LIBXML2_PROVIDER selects the libxml2 library:
    |!Value|!Meaning
    |\c AUTO (default)|uses the system libxml2 if it passes the module's behavior probes; otherwise builds the \
        pinned private copy
    |\c SYSTEM|requires the system libxml2 to pass the probes
    |\c BUNDLED|always builds the pinned private copy

    The private copy (libxml2 2.15.4, pinned by URL and SHA-256) is linked statically into the module with hidden
    symbols; it does not replace or install a system libxml2. See the \c README file in the source distribution for
    details.
*/
